fering that the Nicaraguan people  have experienced as a result of the war  this two-week trip my only exposure was  that rally that we we went to a rally  Masaya which was called Maria mujer was  a festival for the for to celebrate the  mother the Mary Virgin and delegations  came from all over the country to this  rally but several two groups that were  on their way to the rally were ambushed  by the Contras and eleven people were  killed women and children these were not  soldiers at all as which is usual of a  contra attack and so instead of a  festival it was a commemoration and it  was very sorrowful and all the joyful  costumes and decorations no but people  were not smiling whereas whereas they  would have otherwise it was the first  case of a priest having been killed by  the Contras and so this was a big blow  to such a heavy Catholic country and and  it was a real step up in them for for  the atrocities of the contrasts we went  to a resettlement village why would they  have resettlement villages in Nicaragua  I can understand in El Salvador where  people would be coming into the city to  either seeking employment or fear of the  bombing but is there that much a  disruption in the countryside by the  countries that people are coming into  the city  there certainly are as a matter of fact  it's become a big tool by the u.s.  saying well you're taking people from  their homes the center needs to  government is removing you know for  example in mosquito resettlement  programs and and when actually the war  in them in the mountain areas where  people do have their villages and is  disrupt is disrupted these people's  lives and the resettlement has been  going to villages that have been built  by European aid and thus enemies of the  people that go to the villages helping  each other with the materials building  these beautiful homes and we went to  visit one impromptu we were rained out  of where we were supposed to go so we  went to another one and so we didn't  feel like anything had been set up for  us at all and these  villages were the houses were very nice  its little little Swiss type built  houses and and the community was very  tight and the people in the community  did they're protected themselves they  had their own militia to defend  themselves against conscious attacks  because they were still in the war zone  this was up in the northern area  although this wasn't right on the border  where they had lived before about  everybody in the town had experienced  some sort of atrocity or lost a family  member to the Contra battle and so had  felt the need to escape from that area  and so I did meet a wonderful a woman  that I started speaking to while our  group was meeting with the rest of the  people and she said that she would not  go back to where she lived before even  if the war stopped because she enjoyed  this new community and the facilities  they had so much better everyone had  their own outhouse and they had a  farming area and their own animals and  and then she took me back to her house  to show me her house which was a I mean  they're really simple as can be expected  but it was a lot more than she had had  before and then that most people have in  Latin American countries Kathleen how  many of them Rooskies did you see down  there and then nasty Cubans I didn't  meet any Russians I ran into a couple of  Cubans the most people that I ran into  there were Europeans ran into people  from Korea from Germany from England  from France most of the people I ran  into they were from Italy of the 52  organizations that are European  organizations that helped Nicaragua  twelve of those are Italian and most of  them are sponsored by private a private  aid from those countries  they are agricultural and an educational  and health are the three main areas that  the Europeans help Nicaragua and it's  basically solidarity assistance because  they everybody realizes the situation  that Nicaragua is in and our government  seems to be quite isolated in its  opinion of Nicaragua worldwide people on  the on both sides east and west buck are  helping Nicaragua very openly of the  Arias peace plan to look forward to do  you have a sense that that might  actually work in Nicaragua that there is  a possibility for peaceful settlement in  that region yes I think that the Central  American government's getting together  as they have on this plan and even the  right-wing governments and electric  governments coming to an agreement on  what their countries need Central  America has to solve Central America's  problems that's what it comes down to  and our government has to stop  interfering and trying to control  Central America because they're not a  puppet because they have autonomy  they're beautiful countries the people  are wonderful and have a lot more  patience and I think many of us would  under the same circumstances of  oppression and there's a statement made  to me by someone on this trip I can't  remember who it was but they said El  Salvador and Nicaragua are really the  same after I've been going over how much  I don't know how much they contrasted  they said in both countries that people  want freedom  you  [Music]
 

